# The boundary of the fix, seated so that crossing it has to be deliberate.
#
# `scan_encapsed_part_string` serves SIX terminals and this tree now seats
# four. The two it does not are the heredoc pair, and the line is not a matter
# of taste: with `is_heredoc` true the first statement of the function reads
# `scanner->heredocs`, a stack of tags that one token pushes and another
# spends. That is carried state, which is `fence`'s animal and not `marrow`'s,
# and seating it here would be the kotlin defect again - one shape's walk
# quietly answering another shape's terminals.
#
# **So this specimen is expected to FAIL today, and that is its whole job.**
# It is the handover the way `double-quoted-string.php` was one an hour ago:
# the claims below are tree-sitter's tree, and php will hold them when the
# heredoc pair is seated on a fence. Until then the failure is the honest
# report that four of six is what landed.
#
# **It is NOT the guard on the seating, and the first draft of this comment
# said it was.** That draft claimed a later hand widening the marrow roster
# onto the heredoc pair "would make THIS file's `heredoc_body` come out wrong
# rather than absent". It was written as an argument, then run as a falsifier -
# roster widened, binary rebuilt - and it is false. These bytes stop at
# `heredoc_start`, a THIRD unseated external, before either content terminal is
# ever asked for, so the widened build's forest over this file is byte-
# identical to the correct one and this specimen reads 0/7 on both. A red
# specimen cannot report a regression: there is no number below zero for it to
# fall to.
#
# The guard that does hold is `scanner: a row claims the terminals it is
# pinned to and no others` in `scanner_test.zig`, which pins this row's four
# member names and fails naming the two that arrived. It exists because this
# comment was wrong.
#
# Claims are tree-sitter's tree over these bytes.
roots 1
mends 0
holds heredoc
holds heredoc_body
lacks text
spans heredoc 11 23
spans heredoc_body 17 19
